<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Inquiry Project Overview</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00815</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Inquiry Project Overview</md:title>
    <md:uuid>645c5f3f-6b3c-4b3b-a803-787c38849ada</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><div class="os-raise-familysupport">
    <para><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/358ff10b550843649c8b9894394d404490bd0308">Access the activity sheet</link> and provide copies to your students so they can complete the project.</para>
</div>


<para>In this project, students will apply their prior knowledge about plotting coordinate points, evaluating expressions using the correct order of operations, and simplifying algebraic expressions, to generate initial ideas about functions.</para> 

  <para>Students will first be asked to play with a simulation for a few minutes. This open play time allows students to learn how to use a simulation for learning, discover how they can interact with it, and feel ownership of their learning from the simulation. Following open play, students can share how to use the simulation, so every student is aware of the controls and how to interact.</para> 
  
  <para>When students are engaging with the activity sheet, they should be pausing to discuss some of their responses with a partner. The whole class discussions should produce a rich variety of thoughts and observations based on individual responses and partner discussions. </para>
  
<para>  By the end of the lesson, students should have completed the activity sheets using the simulation, and should be able to describe a function as having inputs, outputs, and behaving consistently. They should also be able to evaluate a function using different inputs, and be able to summarize the impact that certain operations have on the algebraic function, the table of values, the appearance of the graph, and the outputs. </para>

<title>Project Activities</title> 
<list type="enumerated">
    <item>Simulating Relationships <emphasis>(10 minutes)</emphasis></item>
    <item>What is a Function? <emphasis>(15 minutes)</emphasis></item>
    <item>Different Kinds of Functions <emphasis>(20 minutes)</emphasis></item>
</list>

<title>Required Preparation</title>
<list type="enumerated">
    <item><link url="https://phet.colorado.edu/sims/html/function-builder/latest/function-builder_en.html?screens=1,2,3">Preview the simulation</link> so that you are able to address questions regarding how to access the activities. </item> 
    <item><link url="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/358ff10b550843649c8b9894394d404490bd0308">Provide copies of the activity sheet</link> for students to use to collect their thinking. </item>
    <item>While each student may have their own technology device, place students into groups of 3-4 so they may discuss their thinking. </item> 
</list>


<title>Required Materials</title>
<list type="enumerated">
    <item>Technology: 2:1 or 1:1 laptop, chromebook, or iPad</item> 
</list>

<table class="os-raise-textheavytable">
    <caption>Learning Objectives</caption>
    <thead>
      <row>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Learning Goals (Teacher Facing)</entry>
        <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Learning Targets (Student Facing)</entry>
      </row>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <row>
        <entry>
            <list>
                <item> Define a function as a rule relating each input to exactly one output and consistently acting on inputs. </item>
                <item>Predict outputs of a function using given inputs.</item>
                <item>Describe a linear function in terms of a table, graph, equation, or series of operations.</item>
            </list>
        </entry>
        <entry><list>
            <item>I can define a function.</item>
            <item>I can predict the output of a function using a given input.</item> 
            <item> I can describe a linear function in terms of a table, graph, equation, or series of operations. </item> 
        </list>
        </entry>
      </row>
    </tbody>
  </table>


<title>Texas Essential Knowledge and Skills (TEKS)</title>
<list>
    <item>ALG1.1(B) Use a problem-solving model that incorporates analyzing given information, formulating a play or strategy, determining a solution, justifying the solution, and evaluating the problem-solving process and the reasonableness of the solution.</item>
    <item>ALG1.1(E) Create and use representations to organize, record, and communicate mathematical ideas. </item> 
    <item>ALG1.12(B) Decide whether relations represented verbally, tabularly, graphically, and symbolically define a function.</item> 
    <item>ALG1.12(C) Evaluate functions, expressed in function notation, given one or more elements in their domains.</item> 
    
</list>






</section>
